---
/**
 * What every lever holds fixed, and the one thing it deliberately does not.
 *
 * # Why this is a component and was markup on one page
 *
 * It lived in `pages/scenario.astro`, and the reason it was written argues for it being on both
 * scenario routes rather than one:
 *
 * > Placed above the controls rather than below the results, because it is a limit on what the
 * > reader is about to do and not a footnote on what they got.
 *
 * `/district/:irn/scenario` renders the same five levers and had none of it. Its own
 * "What this does not move" card names assessed valuation and enrollment — limits specific to that
 * view — and said nothing about the $812.5M of categoricals the base cost lever also moves, nothing
 * about DPIA and targeted assistance cancelling, nothing about gifted or preschool.
 *
 * That route's header comment calls it "the page a school board sends", and `renderDraft` has
 * already made exactly this argument about exactly this page:
 *
 * > A district page is the one a school board sends, and it answers "what would this bill do to us"
 * > with a dollar figure and a per-pupil figure — so a draft's levers applied there with no card
 * > naming the clauses the model cannot reach is the invariant's worst failure, not its mildest.
 *
 * The first version of the draft banner wrote it only in the statewide branch too. Same shape, one
 * card over.
 *
 * # Why the wording did not need a district variant
 *
 * Because the caveat is about the *lever*, not about the view. "Every lever moves all 609 at once
 * and the statewide average is the first thing that would move" is the same sentence on a page
 * reporting one district — that page reports the statewide counts beside its own figure, for the
 * same reason.
 */
import { count, millions, pct } from "../lib/format.ts";
import { heldFixed, refreshEffect, type RefreshEffect } from "../lib/refresh.ts";
import * as routes from "../lib/routes.ts";
import type { District } from "../lib/types.ts";
import { yearChip } from "../lib/year.ts";
import SectionAnchor from "./SectionAnchor.astro";

interface Props {
  /** The whole panel: the figures here are statewide and identical on both routes. */
  districts: District[];
  /** How many districts a lever reaches. */
  total: number;
  /** The refresh the feed prices, already resolved by the caller that also gives it to the levers. */
  refresh: RefreshEffect | null;
}

const { districts, total, refresh } = Astro.props;
// Memoised on the panel, which `loadFeed` caches — so this is summed once per build and not once
// per district page.
const fixed = heldFixed(districts);
---

<div class="card" id="held-fixed" data-part="held-fixed">
  <h2><SectionAnchor id="held-fixed" />What these levers hold fixed, and one they deliberately do not<Fragment set:html={yearChip("formula")} /></h2>
  <p class="note">
    Ohio's formula contains statewide constants — the average base cost per pupil, the statewide
    poverty share, the median weighted wealth — that are computed once over all districts. The
    department's own calculator warns that they are{" "}
    <strong>not recalculated</strong> when a district's data changes. That is right for a tool
    where you change one district and everything else stays put. It is wrong here, where every
    lever moves all {count(total)} at once and the statewide average is the first thing
    that would move.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    So <strong>this model diverges from the department's on purpose</strong>. The{" "}
    <strong>cost input refresh</strong> lever scales base cost <em>and</em> the
    {" "}{millions(fixed.denominated).replace("+", "")} of categorical funding priced in it — special
    education, English learners and career-technical are each a weight times the average base
    cost per pupil, so they rise mechanically when it does.
    {
      refresh && (
        <Fragment>
          {" "}The refresh <a href={routes.wikiNode("draft-legislation", refresh.slug)}>{
            refresh.slug
          }</a> prices — {pct(refresh.scale - 1, 2)} on base cost — delivers{" "}
          {millions(refresh.throughBaseCost).replace("+", "")} through base cost aid and{" "}
          {millions(refresh.throughCategoricals).replace("+", "")} more through those three. Until
          recently this page delivered only the first.
        </Fragment>
      )
    }
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Three things are still held still, for three different reasons. The two biggest categoricals
    — <a href={routes.wikiNode("formula-component", "fsfp-disadvantaged-pupil-impact-aid")}>DPIA</a>{" "}
    and{" "}
    <a href={routes.wikiNode("formula-component", "fsfp-targeted-assistance")}>targeted assistance</a>,
    {" "}{millions(fixed.indexDriven).replace("+", "")} together — divide a district's figure by a
    statewide one, so under a change that moves everything the numerator and denominator move
    together and genuinely cancel.{" "}
    <a href={routes.wikiNode("formula-component", "fsfp-gifted-units")}>Gifted</a> is priced in
    stated salaries rather than in base cost; a refresh would move those too, but by their own
    amount, and scaling them by this factor would be a guess wearing the shape of an identity.
    And the weighted half of{" "}
    <a href={routes.wikiNode("formula-component", "fsfp-preschool-special-education")}>preschool
    special education</a>, {millions(fixed.preschoolWeighted).replace("+", "")}, is denominated the same way
    but sits outside foundation funding — outside everything on this page, not held fixed within
    it.
  </p>
</div>
